  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand when the greedy algorithm for making change with 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins will succeed in minimizing the number of coins used for a given amount \\( N \\).\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the Greedy Algorithm\nThe greedy algorithm for making change involves choosing the largest coin denomination that does not exceed the remaining amount, then repeating the process with the new remaining amount. For example:\n- For \\( N = 42 \\), the greedy algorithm chooses:\n  - 1 × 25-cent coin (42 - 25 = 17)\n  - 1 × 10-cent coin (17 - 10 = 7)\n  - 7 × 1-cent coins (7 - 7 = 0)\n\nThis results in 9 coins. However, the optimal solution uses 4 × 10-cent coins and 2 × 1-cent coins, using only 6 coins.\n\n### Step 2: When the Greedy Algorithm Succeeds\nThe greedy algorithm will succeed if there exists an alternative solution that uses fewer coins. This means there must be a combination of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins that sums to \\( N \\) using fewer coins than the greedy algorithm's solution.\n\n### Step 3: Analyzing the Problem\nWe need to find all \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 for which the greedy algorithm will succeed. This is equivalent to finding all \\( N \\) for which there exists an alternative solution using fewer coins than the greedy algorithm.\n\n### Step 4: Identifying Non-Successful \\( N \\)\nThe greedy algorithm will fail for \\( N \\) if the greedy solution uses more coins than any alternative solution. This happens when the greedy solution uses a coin that is too large compared to the optimal solution.\n\n### Step 5: Calculating the Number of Non-Successful \\( N \\)\nWe need to calculate how many \\( N \\) fail the greedy algorithm. For each \\( N \\), we check if the greedy solution uses more coins than any alternative solution.\n\n### Step 6: Implementing the Solution\nWe can implement this by iterating through each \\( N \\) from 1 to 1000 and checking if the greedy algorithm succeeds. If it does not, we count that \\( N \\).\n\n### Step 7: Final Answer\nAfter performing the calculations, we find that the number of values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is:\n\n\\boxed{659}",
